<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Braganza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1442">1442</a>–<a href="/years/1910">1910</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Braganza dynasty, Braganzas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Braganza was the royal dynasty of Portugal from 1640 to 1910 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> from 1822 to 1889. Descended from an illegitimate son of John I, the dukes of Braganza became the greatest nobles of Portugal, and in 1640 Duke John led the revolt against Spanish rule and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> IV. The family moved to Brazil in 1807–08, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Pedro I</a> became the first Emperor of Brazil. It lost the Portuguese throne in the revolution of 1910.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-braganza.jpg" alt="House of Braganza" width="300" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zulske heraldry · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duchy_of_Braganza_(1640-1910).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The house was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-kongo">Afonso I</a>, 1st <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-of-portugal">Duke of Braganza</a>, an illegitimate son of King John I of Portugal, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aviz">House of Aviz</a>, and Inês Pires. His father valued him and arranged his marriage to Beatriz Pereira de Alvim, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuno-alvares-pereira">Nuno Álvares Pereira</a>, Portugal's most important general and a friend of the king. Afonso was also given the title of Count of Barcelos. The house grew into one of the wealthiest and most powerful noble houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By 1640 Portugal was overtaxed, its colonies were unprotected, and Philip III of Portugal had lost the trust of most of the nobility and was loathed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of merchants. The Braganzas deposed the Philippine Dynasty in the Restoration War, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-of-portugal">Duke of Braganza</a> became King John IV. In 1807, facing a Napoleonic invasion, the royal court moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, which was made a kingdom alongside Portugal, forming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-kingdom-of-portugal-brazil-and-the-algarves">United Kingdom of Portugal, Brazil and the Algarves</a>. João VI returned to Portugal in 1821.</p><p>On 7 September 1822 his son Pedro, regent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, declared independence and was acclaimed Emperor of Brazil. Pedro I abdicated in 1831 in favour of his young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a>, who was declared of age in 1840 and brought stability to a country on the verge of disintegration. Under him Brazil won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/platine-war">Platine War</a>, the Uruguayan War and the Paraguayan War, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, restricted by successive laws, was abolished in 1888 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabel-princess-imperial-of-brazil">Princess Isabel</a>.</p><p>In Portugal, Pedro became King Pedro IV in 1826 and abdicated in favour of his seven-year-old daughter Maria, with his brother Miguel as regent. In 1828 Miguel instead proclaimed himself king and repudiated the liberal constitution. Maria II later reigned, and the house continued in Portugal until Manuel II was deposed in 1910.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The house produced 15 Portuguese monarchs and all four Brazilian monarchs, and consorts to several European kingdoms, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-of-braganza">Catherine of Braganza</a>, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-england">Charles II of England</a>. Its crest is a green wyvern, taken to represent Saint George, and it appears on the Portuguese Crown Sceptre and the Sceptre of the Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. A tradition, the Curse of the Braganzas, holds that after John IV mistreated a Franciscan friar no first-born son of the dynasty would live to reign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Braganza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The coup ended 60 years of union with Spain; the <b>Braganza dynasty</b> reigned until 1910.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-1">1 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
